{"type":"WebSearch","query":"2003 California recall results Schwarzenegger Bustamante percentages","content":"**Arnold Schwarzenegger (Republican) received 48.58% (4,206,284 votes) and Cruz Bustamante (Democrat) received 31.47% (2,724,874 votes) in the replacement candidate portion of the 2003 California gubernatorial recall election.**[[1]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2003_California_gubernatorial_recall_election)

The recall of incumbent Gov. Gray Davis passed with 55.39% yes votes. Schwarzenegger won a plurality among the 135 replacement candidates (Tom McClintock placed third at 13.41%). These are the official certified results. Early unofficial tallies (e.g., with 100% precincts reporting shortly after Election Day) showed slightly different rounded figures around 49% and 32%.[[2]](https://www.sfgate.com/politics/article/CALIFORNIA-RECALL-How-each-candidate-fared-2571892.php)

The election was held October 7, 2003, with results certified later that month.","citations":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2003_California_gubernatorial_recall_election","https://www.sfgate.com/politics/article/CALIFORNIA-RECALL-How-each-candidate-fared-2571892.php"],"allowed_domains":null}

{"type":"WebSearch","query":"2021 California recall Larry Elder percentage of all ballots replacement vote","content":"**Larry Elder received 48.4% of the votes cast in the replacement candidate contest (3,563,867 votes out of 7,361,568 valid votes for a replacement).** This was the highest share among the 46 candidates.[[1]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_California_gubernatorial_recall_election)

This figure is the standard reported percentage for the second question on the ballot (“If Newsom is recalled, who should replace him?”). All voters could answer it regardless of their vote on the recall itself. Democrats largely followed advice to skip the second question, so far fewer people voted on replacements than on the yes/no recall (about 57% of ballots included a replacement choice; 42.9% were blank/invalid on that question).[[2]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/California_2021_recall_election)

If measured against *all* ballots cast in the election (12,892,578 total), Elder’s share was about 27.6%. Early incomplete counts were sometimes reported around 26%. The recall itself failed, with 61.88% voting “No” (Newsom stayed in office). Results were certified in October 2021.[[3]](https://elections.cdn.sos.ca.gov/sov/2021-recall/sov/11-sov-summary.pdf)","citations":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_California_gubernatorial_recall_election","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/California_2021_recall_election","https://elections.cdn.sos.ca.gov/sov/2021-recall/sov/11-sov-summary.pdf"],"allowed_domains":null}
